---
Scope: SPEC.md D6/D7 (lines 28-29), WS5 acceptance (lines 200-205), rulings
2026-09-24c through g (attempt-4 contract: "word every persons/start-date 400
from the submitted value", ruling g). Reviewed .swarm/diffs/WS5.4.diff against
snapshot (no .git copy, no server contacted, no budget2 binary run from a repo
root).

ATTACK: "names with quotes/regex metacharacters/unicode" (explicitly named in
dispatch). rePersonsInvalidBirthMonth (new in this attempt, added to satisfy
ruling g's missing-vs-unparseable distinction) is:
  `^persons: invalid birth_month for "([^"]*)": .+$`
It extracts the person's Name by regex-parsing prepare.ValidatePersons' %q-
escaped error text back out. Go's %q escapes an embedded `"` in the name as
`\"` -- a literal backslash then a literal quote character -- which the
`[^"]*` capture class cannot skip over. For any Name containing a literal
double-quote (an ordinary, unrestricted `<input type="text"
name="person_name[]">`, no `pattern` attribute, confirmed in
rate-assumptions.html and whatif-rate-assumptions.js -- e.g. a real nickname
like `Robert "Bob" Smith`), the regex fails to match entirely and
personsSaveErrorMessage falls through to the generic
`"Invalid person data: " + msg` branch -- the SAME branch the contract
reserves for classes it deliberately leaves unpolished (duplicate id, name
required, wrong primary count), not for the empty/unparseable birth-month
classes ruling g explicitly requires to be worded "<Name> needs a birth
month" / "<Name>'s birth month "<value>" isn't a valid month".

Reproduced at both the unit level (personsSaveErrorMessage direct call) and
end-to-end through handleWhatIfSettings via a real form POST (temp test files
in my snapshot only, run with GOMAXPROCS=4 GOFLAGS="-count=1 -p=2", removed
after use; worker's own suite re-run clean afterward: `go test
./internal/handlers/whatif/...` -> ok, 16.5s):

POST person_name[]="Robert \"Bob\" Smith", person_birth_month[]="" ->
status=400 (never 500, that invariant holds), body:
  "Invalid person data: persons: invalid birth_month for
  &#34;Robert \&#34;Bob\&#34; Smith&#34;: month is required"
-- not "Robert \"Bob\" Smith needs a birth month" as ruling g requires for
this exact class (submitted birth month genuinely empty). The raw Go
internal error text ("month is required" wrapped inside the ValidatePersons
message) is what actually reaches the user, in place of the specified
wording.

This is NOT a false "missing" claim and NOT a wrong-person attribution (both
of those I also probed and found sound -- see below) -- it is a silent
fallback out of the two classes the contract mandates specific wording for,
triggered by input the person-name field imposes no restriction against. It
lands inside this attempt's own written scope: rePersonsInvalidBirthMonth and
its name round-trip through fmt.Errorf(%q)+regexp are new code this attempt
added specifically to implement ruling g.

Other probes on the same attack surface, all held (no defect):
- Duplicate names, one candidate's submitted birth month empty and one
  non-empty: does NOT falsely say "needs a birth month" (conservative
  fallback "isn't valid", per the classifier's own allEmpty check).
- Unicode name (accented + CJK, no embedded quote): correctly named,
  "needs a birth month" -- %q does not escape printable non-ASCII runes.
- Regex-metacharacter name (`A.B*C(D)`, no quote): correctly named -- match
  against `p.Name == name` is a literal string compare, not a compiled
  regex, so metacharacters in the VALUE are inert.
- Whitespace-only submitted birth month: correctly classified as "needs a
  birth month" (TrimSpace).
- "birth_month after start_date" collision with duplicate/reordered values:
  algebraically the first string-match necessarily lands on the same person
  ValidatePersons itself failed on first (re-derived independently; attempt-3
  second checker proved the same for that pre-existing branch).
- "persons:" vs "start_date:" prefixes are mutually exclusive by construction
  (ValidatePersons' own fmt.Errorf call sites) -- no mislabelling either way.
- HTML escaping: renderError's html.EscapeString covers this new path too
  (confirmed: raw `"` -> `&#34;` in the actual response body above); no XSS.

D6 spot-check (unchanged from attempt 3, not the target of this attempt's
contract): worker's own suite green, no re-probe beyond that given the
attempt-4 contract is scoped to the persons/start-date wording.

Remedy check (rule 6b): fixable without exceeding this task's scope -- stop
round-tripping the person's name through a %q-formatted error string and
back out via regex; the handler already has the SUBMITTED persons slice
(used for the empty/unparseable/name decision itself) and does not need to
recover the name from the error text at all, or the regex needs to correctly
un-escape Go's %q syntax before comparing.
